<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">League of Corinth</h1><p class="st-meta">337 BCE – 322 BCE (approx.) · capital Corinth</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alliance of Greek states under Macedonian leadership created by Philip II after Chaeronea, the framework for Alexander the Great's invasion of Persia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After defeating <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Chaeronea</a> in 338 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> called the Greek states to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a> in 338-337 BCE. They swore to a common peace, forbidding wars and revolutions among members, and named Philip hegemon of a war of revenge against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> alone refused to join.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council (synedrion) of delegates, with votes roughly proportional to each member's military strength, met at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a> and other festival sites and could judge disputes. Members kept their own governments, but Macedonian garrisons held Corinth, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ambracia">Ambracia</a>. Alexander had his leadership confirmed in 336 BCE and punished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>' revolt in 335 BCE in the league's name.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The alliance gave Alexander a Greek mandate for his Asian campaign. On news of his death in 323 BCE, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamian-war">Lamian War</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crannon">Crannon</a> in 322 BCE ended both the war and the league. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and Demetrius briefly revived it in 302 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/league-of-corinth.jpg" alt="League of Corinth" width="320" height="257"><figcaption>Image: Map_Macedonia_336_BC-es.svg: Marsyas (French original); Kordas (Spanish translation) derivative work: MinisterForBadT... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Macedonia_336_BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>337 BCE – 322 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corinth</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip II of Macedon, Alexander the Great, Antipater</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hellenic League</dd></dl></aside></div>

